    REST - Representational State Transfer - an architectural style for designing networked
           applications/APIs around resources and a uniform interface
               Constraints:
                   * Client-server     - client and server have separate responsibilities
                   * Stateless         - each request contains all info needed to process it;
                                         server does not store client session state between \
                                         requests
                   * Cacheable         - responses must indicate whether they can be cached;
                                         caching can improve performance and reduce server load
                   * Uniform interface - clients interact with resources through a consistent,
                                         standardized interface that includes:
                                             * resource identification
                                             * manipulation through representation
                                             * self-descriptive messages
                                             * HATEOAS
                   * Layered system    - client cannot necessarily tell whether it is
                                         communicating directly with the server or through
                                         intermediary layers like proxies, gateways or load
                                         balancers
                   * Code-on-demand (optional) - server may send executable code to the client


    HTTP methods:
        * GET     - read
        * POST    - create
        * PUT     - update/create if not exists
        * PATCH   - partially update
        * DELETE  - delete
        * HEAD    - returns headers same as GET would; for checking metadata without body
        * OPTIONS - asks what communication options/methods are available
        * QUERY   - new since 2026; performs server side query using content in request body


    HTTP status codes:
        * 200 - OK
        * 201 - Created
        * 204 - No Content
        * 400 - Bad Request
        * 401 - Unauthorized
        * 403 - Forbidden
        * 404 - Not Found
        * 409 - Conflict
        * 415 Unsupported Media Type
        * 500 - Internal Server Error
        * 503 - Service Unavailable


    Idempotency - idempotent operation can be performed multiple times and the intended effect on
                  the server is the same as if it had been performed once
                  Idempotent methods:
                      * GET
                      * PUT
                      * DELETE
                  Non-idempotent methods:
                      * POST (sending twice would create 2 resources)
                      * PATCH


    Content negotiation
        * Content-Type header - tells the server what format the request body is in
        * Accept header       - tells the server what response format client wants
